1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,520
Okay, next discussion concerns the small bowel.

2
00:00:02,660 --> 00:00:04,640
The first disorder is duodenal atresia.

3
00:00:04,760 --> 00:00:08,760
Again, when you have something that's atretic, it ends in a blind loop.

4
00:00:08,860 --> 00:00:12,040
And so basically the idea here is that the duodenum ends in a blind loop.

5
00:00:12,360 --> 00:00:14,160
The reason why we get this is congenital,

6
00:00:14,440 --> 00:00:17,080
and it's failure of the small bowel to actually canalize,

7
00:00:17,420 --> 00:00:19,080
highly associated with Down syndrome.

8
00:00:19,220 --> 00:00:21,860
That's a high-yield association for examination purposes.

9
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:25,600
Now the clinical features are all based on the fact that you're going to have this blind loop.

10
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:29,200
So for example, this is the esophagus, and then you've got the stomach coming off,

11
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:30,840
and then eventually you have the duodenum.

12
00:00:31,140 --> 00:00:32,760
The duodenum is ending in a blind loop.

13
00:00:33,140 --> 00:00:35,700
So one problem that's going to occur is polyhydramnios.

14
00:00:35,820 --> 00:00:38,220
Now polyhydramnios means too much amniotic fluid.

15
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:43,140
Remember that the amniotic fluid, the major component, is generated from the urine of the baby,

16
00:00:43,340 --> 00:00:47,100
so that the urine of the baby is the major component of the amniotic fluid.

17
00:00:47,420 --> 00:00:50,740
And at the same time, the way by which we resorb some of that amniotic fluid

18
00:00:50,740 --> 00:00:52,860
is by swallowing it and digesting it,

19
00:00:52,860 --> 00:00:56,020
so that the baby is swallowing and digesting amniotic fluid,

20
00:00:56,140 --> 00:00:58,640
and that helps to decrease the volume of amniotic fluid.

21
00:00:58,640 --> 00:01:01,680
If we don't have that physiologic swallowing and digesting,

22
00:01:01,680 --> 00:01:04,380
we're going to get too much amniotic fluid, or polyhydramnios.

23
00:01:04,700 --> 00:01:08,680
And of course, in this particular case, the babies can bring in a little bit of that amniotic fluid,

24
00:01:08,840 --> 00:01:10,880
but they can't pass it through the rest of the gut.

25
00:01:11,040 --> 00:01:14,360
So this would be one problem that could arise in these babies.

26
00:01:14,780 --> 00:01:17,160
Another problem is that they get bilious vomiting,

27
00:01:17,320 --> 00:01:21,380
and the idea here is that as you get building up of the food within this tract,

28
00:01:21,440 --> 00:01:22,960
of course it ends in a blind loop,

29
00:01:22,960 --> 00:01:25,720
and of course the bile is coming in from the duodenum,

30
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:28,840
and so this is going to end up being regurgitated out,

31
00:01:28,960 --> 00:01:30,260
and that's going to result in vomiting,

32
00:01:30,440 --> 00:01:34,540
and it'll be bilious because it's got bile coming in at the level of the duodenum.

33
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:36,300
So that's an important clinical feature.

34
00:01:36,400 --> 00:01:39,760
And the third important clinical feature is that you're going to get distension of the stomach,

35
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:42,560
and you're also going to get distension of the duodenum.

36
00:01:42,560 --> 00:01:47,680
And in between here is the pyloric sphincter, which is going to remain tight.

37
00:01:47,940 --> 00:01:52,680
And so that's going to produce two separate areas of distension,

38
00:01:53,080 --> 00:01:54,980
and that is called the double bubble sign.

39
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:56,800
It's best visualized by imaging.

40
00:01:57,140 --> 00:01:58,960
So here you've got the stomach, for example.

41
00:01:59,340 --> 00:02:03,560
There's a tightening here where you have the pyloric sphincter,

42
00:02:04,280 --> 00:02:07,020
and then here you've got the duodenum,

43
00:02:07,220 --> 00:02:10,500
so two bubbles, bubble number one and then bubble number two,

44
00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:13,880
double bubble sign, very high yield for examination purposes.

45
00:02:14,980 --> 00:02:19,640
Meckel's diverticulum, remember that a diverticulum is an outpouching of a wall.

46
00:02:19,900 --> 00:02:20,820
That's the first point.

47
00:02:21,100 --> 00:02:24,640
And in this particular case, you get outpouching of all of the layers of the ball wall.

48
00:02:24,900 --> 00:02:27,160
So that's called a true diverticulum.

49
00:02:27,520 --> 00:02:29,540
Remember that you can also have false diverticulums.

50
00:02:29,620 --> 00:02:33,200
For example, the one diverticulum that we've covered in this chapter so far

51
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:34,580
was a Zenker's diverticulum,

52
00:02:34,580 --> 00:02:38,420
in which you had outpouching of the mucosa through the muscle wall,

53
00:02:38,680 --> 00:02:39,760
or through the muscle layer.

54
00:02:39,760 --> 00:02:42,140
And so that was a false diverticulum.

55
00:02:42,440 --> 00:02:44,020
This is a true diverticulum,

56
00:02:44,100 --> 00:02:47,980
and it's one of the few true diverticulums that we're going to study.

57
00:02:48,620 --> 00:02:53,120
Now this arises embryologically due to a failure of the vitalin duct to involute.

58
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:55,260
Let's talk about the vitalin duct for a moment.

59
00:02:55,880 --> 00:02:58,080
Remember that in early embryonic development,

60
00:02:58,440 --> 00:03:01,960
the midgut actually receives its nutrients from the yolk sac.

61
00:03:02,260 --> 00:03:05,460
So early in life, when we're developing the baby or the fetus,

62
00:03:05,460 --> 00:03:09,160
the midgut is actually going to receive its nutrients from the yolk sac.

63
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:13,180
And the way by which it receives those nutrients is via the vitalin duct.

64
00:03:13,300 --> 00:03:18,880
So we could say that the vitalin duct is vital for receiving nutrients during early development.

65
00:03:19,260 --> 00:03:21,440
This duct normally forms in about the fourth week.

66
00:03:21,720 --> 00:03:24,500
It then involutes by about the seventh week of gestation.

67
00:03:24,840 --> 00:03:26,780
And if you have persistence of the duct,

68
00:03:27,060 --> 00:03:29,640
that results in something called a Meckel's diverticulum.

69
00:03:29,640 --> 00:03:31,960
So it's really persistence of the vitalin duct.

70
00:03:32,300 --> 00:03:34,460
It's interesting, you can have a couple problems here.

71
00:03:34,720 --> 00:03:39,900
One problem is that you can have the fact that the duct does not involute at all,

72
00:03:40,200 --> 00:03:44,480
and that would result in the passing of meconium via the umbilicus at birth.

73
00:03:44,740 --> 00:03:50,820
So if they gave you an examination question in which the patient is passing meconium via the umbilicus,

74
00:03:50,960 --> 00:03:55,860
that means that there was failure of the vitalin duct to resolve completely,

75
00:03:55,860 --> 00:04:00,640
meaning that there was no resorption or no involution of that duct at all.

76
00:04:00,980 --> 00:04:04,420
But it's also possible that you can get a slight closing of that duct,

77
00:04:04,520 --> 00:04:06,320
but it fails to involute completely,

78
00:04:06,480 --> 00:04:09,100
and that would result in something called a Meckel's diverticulum.

79
00:04:09,420 --> 00:04:12,540
One of the interesting things that may be seen, especially on examination,

80
00:04:12,700 --> 00:04:14,380
when you do a clinical exam on these patients,

81
00:04:14,740 --> 00:04:18,820
is you can kind of feel the umbilical area, and it feels like there's stool.

82
00:04:18,980 --> 00:04:21,540
It feels like there's something firm but soft,

83
00:04:21,960 --> 00:04:25,040
or something solid but soft within the umbilical area,

84
00:04:25,040 --> 00:04:27,780
and that's because there's stool caught up in the Meckel's diverticulum.

85
00:04:28,160 --> 00:04:30,900
So that's an important, interesting finding as well.

86
00:04:31,940 --> 00:04:34,860
Now, this is what the Meckel's diverticulum looks like.

87
00:04:35,080 --> 00:04:37,260
This is basically the diverticulum here.

88
00:04:37,460 --> 00:04:39,820
It's an outpouching coming off the intestine,

89
00:04:39,920 --> 00:04:41,800
and the intestine, of course, would be running this way.

90
00:04:42,200 --> 00:04:45,240
Now, there's some rules of twos that are highly associated with the Meckel's,

91
00:04:45,240 --> 00:04:46,700
and you do need to be familiar with them.

92
00:04:46,700 --> 00:04:52,080
They help you to remember some of the important clinical and epidemiologic features.

93
00:04:52,080 --> 00:04:54,580
Number one, it's seen in 2% of the population.

94
00:04:54,780 --> 00:04:58,120
It's the most common congenital anomaly of the GI tract.

95
00:04:58,340 --> 00:05:01,360
It's usually 2 inches long, and it's usually located in the small bowel,

96
00:05:01,620 --> 00:05:04,540
about 2 feet from the ileocecal valve.

97
00:05:04,880 --> 00:05:09,260
And again, by here, you can imagine that this would be 2 inches long.

98
00:05:09,260 --> 00:05:11,640
So those are the rules of twos.

99
00:05:11,640 --> 00:05:12,480
There's a few more.

100
00:05:12,480 --> 00:05:16,220
First of all, this usually occurs, usually it's asymptomatic,

101
00:05:16,220 --> 00:05:19,820
but if it does present, it can present within the first two years of life.

102
00:05:19,820 --> 00:05:23,800
And the way by which it can present includes bleeding, volvulus,

103
00:05:23,800 --> 00:05:28,060
and a volvulus is where you get a twisting of the bowel on its mesentery.

104
00:05:28,060 --> 00:05:29,860
It can present with intussusception,

105
00:05:29,860 --> 00:05:32,980
and intussusception is when you get telescoping of the bowel,

106
00:05:32,980 --> 00:05:34,660
or it can present with obstruction.

107
00:05:35,200 --> 00:05:36,840
This bleeding is particularly important,

108
00:05:36,840 --> 00:05:41,420
and that's because you need to know that within a Meckel's eye reticulum,

109
00:05:41,420 --> 00:05:43,880
you can also have heterotopic gastric mucosa.

110
00:05:43,880 --> 00:05:46,000
Now, that gastric mucosa would produce acid,

111
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:49,180
and then you'd get acidic damage to the Meckel's eye reticulum,

112
00:05:49,180 --> 00:05:50,880
which would then present as bleeding.

113
00:05:52,100 --> 00:05:54,300
All right, next disorder is a volvulus.

114
00:05:55,300 --> 00:05:58,000
This is when you get twisting of the bowel along its mesentery.

115
00:05:58,180 --> 00:05:59,880
Now, remember that if you've got bowel,

116
00:05:59,880 --> 00:06:02,380
the blood supply is coming in through the mesentery.

117
00:06:02,500 --> 00:06:05,080
So if you twist the bowel along its mesentery,

118
00:06:05,080 --> 00:06:06,380
you're going to create two problems.

119
00:06:06,380 --> 00:06:08,380
Number one, you're going to obstruct the bowel,

120
00:06:08,380 --> 00:06:10,960
and number two, you're going to disrupt the blood supply.

121
00:06:10,960 --> 00:06:14,420
And of course, a disruption of blood supply will result in infarction.

122
00:06:14,420 --> 00:06:16,420
Now, the most common locations are those locations

123
00:06:16,420 --> 00:06:17,920
where you have a ton of mesentery,

124
00:06:17,920 --> 00:06:21,020
so that there's some redundancy that allows for this twisting.

125
00:06:21,020 --> 00:06:26,220
So in the elderly, the classic location is the sigmoid colon.

126
00:06:26,220 --> 00:06:30,220
And in the young adult, the classic location is the cecum.

127
00:06:30,220 --> 00:06:31,540
Here's what a volvulus looks like.

128
00:06:31,540 --> 00:06:33,700
Basically, this area here has been twisted,

129
00:06:34,120 --> 00:06:37,280
and this area is normal, and this area is normal as well.

130
00:06:37,280 --> 00:06:40,120
And so when this twisted, there was infarction of that area,

131
00:06:40,240 --> 00:06:43,700
and you can clearly see that this is a hemorrhagic infarction of the bowel.

132
00:06:44,620 --> 00:06:48,240
Now, intussusception refers to telescoping of a proximal segment of bowel

133
00:06:48,240 --> 00:06:49,300
into the distal segment.

134
00:06:49,680 --> 00:06:50,740
Now, here's basically the idea.

135
00:06:50,740 --> 00:06:55,200
Let's pretend that this is the bowel, and the flow is moving this way.

136
00:06:55,200 --> 00:07:00,180
The idea is that you kind of drag in the bowel along the direction of flow,

137
00:07:00,320 --> 00:07:02,200
along the direction of peristalsis.

138
00:07:02,600 --> 00:07:03,860
So that's called intussusception,

139
00:07:04,100 --> 00:07:08,180
the telescoping of a proximal segment into a distal segment.

140
00:07:08,740 --> 00:07:11,300
Now, what's important to note about intussusception is that,

141
00:07:11,580 --> 00:07:14,160
obviously, when you drag in the bowel,

142
00:07:14,280 --> 00:07:16,400
you're going to be dragging in the blood supply as well.

143
00:07:17,160 --> 00:07:19,960
And so that dragging in of the blood supply is going to result in infarction.

144
00:07:19,960 --> 00:07:24,100
And these patients classically present with obstruction or with infarction,

145
00:07:24,240 --> 00:07:28,860
and that infarction can often present itself with something called current jelly stools.

146
00:07:29,600 --> 00:07:32,180
Now, it's important to note that whenever you have intussusception,

147
00:07:32,420 --> 00:07:34,420
usually it's associated with a leading edge.

148
00:07:34,720 --> 00:07:38,000
Now, remember I said that basically it's in the direction of peristalsis.

149
00:07:38,300 --> 00:07:41,880
So there's got to be something that the peristalsis can kind of hook on

150
00:07:41,880 --> 00:07:44,260
and grab the bowel and drag it forward.

151
00:07:44,640 --> 00:07:47,860
And so you want to ask yourself the question when you have an intussusception is,

152
00:07:47,860 --> 00:07:52,760
what was the underlying cause that allowed the bowel to get dragged forward?

153
00:07:53,200 --> 00:07:56,580
And in children, the most common cause is lymphoid hyperplasia.

154
00:07:57,020 --> 00:08:01,060
So remember that there are a ton of perist patches within the intestine,

155
00:08:01,220 --> 00:08:06,260
and those perist patches are particularly prevalent within the terminal ilium.

156
00:08:06,560 --> 00:08:08,720
So the idea is the patient gets some sort of,

157
00:08:08,720 --> 00:08:10,740
or the child gets some sort of viral infection,

158
00:08:10,840 --> 00:08:12,440
they get massive lymphoid hyperplasia,

159
00:08:12,440 --> 00:08:16,940
and the terminal ilium gets dragged into the cecum because of the thickness of its wall.

160
00:08:17,940 --> 00:08:21,940
In adults, you'd classically think about tumor as being the leading edge

161
00:08:21,940 --> 00:08:24,640
that allows for an intussusception to occur.

162
00:08:25,360 --> 00:08:26,420
Small bowel infarction,

163
00:08:26,660 --> 00:08:30,300
we should know that the small bowel is very susceptible to ischemic injury.

164
00:08:30,640 --> 00:08:33,200
Remember that the small bowel needs a lot of ATP

165
00:08:33,200 --> 00:08:34,960
because it's doing a ton of digestion.

166
00:08:35,220 --> 00:08:36,440
It does a lot of the absorption

167
00:08:36,440 --> 00:08:40,940
and a lot of the digestion necessary to be able to bring nutrients into the body.

168
00:08:40,940 --> 00:08:44,520
And so it's going to be highly susceptible to ischemic injury.

169
00:08:45,480 --> 00:08:47,380
Now when you get ischemia of the small bowel,

170
00:08:47,480 --> 00:08:49,380
there's a couple possibilities of what could occur.

171
00:08:49,920 --> 00:08:52,480
One possibility is that you can get a transmural infarction.

172
00:08:52,860 --> 00:08:53,840
And this is going to occur,

173
00:08:53,940 --> 00:08:56,820
let's pretend that this is the bowel and this is the blood supply coming in,

174
00:08:57,260 --> 00:08:59,080
and this is the blood supply coming out.

175
00:08:59,140 --> 00:09:00,760
So you've got an artery and you've got a vein.

176
00:09:01,240 --> 00:09:03,620
Now, transmural infarction is going to occur

177
00:09:03,620 --> 00:09:07,340
when you get thrombosis or embolism of the artery,

178
00:09:07,340 --> 00:09:12,220
and it's got to be the superior mesenteric artery,

179
00:09:12,720 --> 00:09:14,820
or if you get thrombosis of the vein.

180
00:09:14,880 --> 00:09:17,340
It's kind of that same paradigm that we keep talking about.

181
00:09:17,700 --> 00:09:18,800
Now let's ask a couple questions.

182
00:09:18,920 --> 00:09:21,360
How would we get embolism of the artery?

183
00:09:21,520 --> 00:09:23,980
Well, one way by which we could get embolism would be

184
00:09:23,980 --> 00:09:25,620
if the patient has atrial fib.

185
00:09:26,160 --> 00:09:28,040
Remember, if the patient has atrial fibrillation,

186
00:09:28,200 --> 00:09:31,660
then basically the idea would be that they can develop a thrombus

187
00:09:31,660 --> 00:09:34,480
because of the lack of motility of the atrium,

188
00:09:34,480 --> 00:09:37,100
and that thrombus could break off and then embolize,

189
00:09:37,180 --> 00:09:38,780
and that would be called a thromboembolist,

190
00:09:38,900 --> 00:09:42,920
and that could lodge itself in the SMA and then create an infarction.

191
00:09:43,480 --> 00:09:47,100
The other possibility would be that the patient, for example, has a vasculitis.

192
00:09:47,580 --> 00:09:49,220
And so if the patient gets a vasculitis,

193
00:09:49,320 --> 00:09:52,400
that can increase the risk for thrombosis and subsequent infarction.

194
00:09:53,060 --> 00:09:57,400
For example, one classic example would be polyarteritis nodosa,

195
00:09:57,800 --> 00:10:00,660
in which the patient's actually had melanoma and abdominal pain.

196
00:10:00,860 --> 00:10:03,060
And if you go back and review polyarteritis nodosa,

197
00:10:03,060 --> 00:10:08,980
you'll see that one of the features was abdominal complaints.

198
00:10:10,060 --> 00:10:13,320
And then the third way would be to get thrombosis of the mesenteric vein.

199
00:10:13,640 --> 00:10:15,700
And how would you get thrombosis of the mesenteric vein?

200
00:10:15,980 --> 00:10:19,260
Well, one classic mechanism would be something called polycythemia vera,

201
00:10:19,480 --> 00:10:21,000
where you have a red blood cell disorder,

202
00:10:21,080 --> 00:10:22,780
where you're producing too many red blood cells,

203
00:10:22,820 --> 00:10:26,640
which makes the blood thick and increases the risk for thrombosis.

204
00:10:26,960 --> 00:10:30,480
Another classic example would be if the patient has a lupus anticoagulant.

205
00:10:30,480 --> 00:10:33,380
That would increase the risk for thrombosis as well.

206
00:10:34,120 --> 00:10:36,840
So in these particular cases, when you're going to block the blood supply,

207
00:10:37,080 --> 00:10:41,180
that's going to result in a transmural infarction so that the entire wall gets infarcted.

208
00:10:41,500 --> 00:10:44,480
Now the other possibility is that you can get a mucosal infarction.

209
00:10:44,920 --> 00:10:48,880
And the way that by which that would occur is that if you've got this blood supply going to the wall,

210
00:10:48,940 --> 00:10:53,400
and you've got a mucosa and a submucosa, etc., and here's the blood supply,

211
00:10:53,400 --> 00:10:57,380
imagine that you don't cut the blood supply completely, but that you decrease it slightly.

212
00:10:57,740 --> 00:11:01,440
If you decrease it slightly, then the mucosa, which is furthest away from the blood supply,

213
00:11:01,760 --> 00:11:06,400
would actually become ischemic, and you'd get infarction only of the mucosa.

214
00:11:06,720 --> 00:11:08,400
And that occurs when you have marked hypotension.

215
00:11:09,140 --> 00:11:12,060
So when there's hypotension, then you can get mucosal infarction

216
00:11:12,060 --> 00:11:14,940
instead of a complete transmural infarction.

217
00:11:16,040 --> 00:11:19,540
The clinical features, of course, will be abdominal pain with bloody diarrhea.

218
00:11:19,540 --> 00:11:24,240
These are the key features of infarction, and there will also be decreased bowel sounds

219
00:11:24,240 --> 00:11:25,960
as the bowel becomes less active.

220
00:11:26,520 --> 00:11:29,320
The next disorder we want to discuss is lactose intolerance.

221
00:11:29,460 --> 00:11:33,220
This is due to a decreased function of the lactase enzyme

222
00:11:33,220 --> 00:11:36,180
that's present in the brush border of the enterocytes.

223
00:11:36,520 --> 00:11:39,380
Before we can understand this, we need to do a little review of physiology.

224
00:11:39,820 --> 00:11:43,040
Let's remind ourselves that one of the nutrients that we want to take from the gut

225
00:11:43,040 --> 00:11:45,880
and bring into the blood is going to be sugar or carbohydrates.

226
00:11:45,880 --> 00:11:50,720
Remember that carbohydrates are broken down into oligosaccharides by the amylases

227
00:11:50,720 --> 00:11:55,740
in the salivary gland and the pancreas, and also into disaccharides,

228
00:11:56,100 --> 00:11:58,140
such as lactose and sucrose.

229
00:11:58,680 --> 00:12:01,660
Now, eventually, you can only absorb monosaccharides,

230
00:12:01,740 --> 00:12:04,400
and so you've got to break down all of those oligosaccharides,

231
00:12:04,480 --> 00:12:08,640
and you've got to break down the lactose and the sucrose as well into monosaccharides.

232
00:12:08,700 --> 00:12:11,840
And remember from biochemistry that there are three monosaccharides

233
00:12:11,840 --> 00:12:15,120
that are going to be absorbed, glucose, galactose, and fructose.

234
00:12:15,120 --> 00:12:19,960
Now, in order to break down lactose, we have an enzyme present on the brush border

235
00:12:19,960 --> 00:12:24,140
of our enterocytes, which line the small bowel, and that is called lactase.

236
00:12:24,180 --> 00:12:26,860
And you need this lactase enzyme to break down lactose.

237
00:12:27,240 --> 00:12:30,200
If there is a decreased function of the lactase enzyme,

238
00:12:30,500 --> 00:12:33,360
then what's going to happen is that the lactose will remain

239
00:12:33,360 --> 00:12:37,620
within the lumen of the small bowel, and lactose is osmotically active.

240
00:12:37,720 --> 00:12:40,540
It will draw water towards it, and that will create a diarrhea.

241
00:12:41,100 --> 00:12:42,280
So how will these patients present?

242
00:12:42,280 --> 00:12:46,240
They present with abdominal distension and diarrhea when they consume milk products,

243
00:12:46,300 --> 00:12:49,740
and that's because milk products contain a ton of lactose.

244
00:12:50,060 --> 00:12:53,060
Now, when you get deficiency, it can be congenital or acquired.

245
00:12:53,420 --> 00:12:55,340
The congenital deficiency is rare.

246
00:12:55,760 --> 00:13:01,160
It would basically be a child who was born without the enzyme.

247
00:13:01,260 --> 00:13:01,980
That's super rare.

248
00:13:02,360 --> 00:13:05,800
You can also get acquired deficiency, and acquired deficiency occurs in two ways.

249
00:13:06,160 --> 00:13:09,620
One way by which patients get acquired deficiency is simply as they age,

250
00:13:09,620 --> 00:13:14,460
they just become intolerant, and this classically would occur, for example, in a child,

251
00:13:14,620 --> 00:13:18,880
so maybe a 10-year-old, 15-year-old child who was normal up to that age,

252
00:13:19,080 --> 00:13:21,180
and then all of a sudden becomes lactose intolerant.

253
00:13:21,220 --> 00:13:23,920
And, of course, they would be lactose intolerant for the rest of their life.

254
00:13:24,620 --> 00:13:28,680
Another acquired way to get lactose intolerant would be after any GI infection.

255
00:13:28,800 --> 00:13:32,880
When you get a GI tract infection, for example, a viral infection of the small bowel,

256
00:13:32,880 --> 00:13:39,280
that can destroy the enzyme, and therefore the patients can become lactose intolerant,

257
00:13:39,380 --> 00:13:43,640
but, of course, that would be temporary until the small bowel would actually heal.

258
00:13:44,460 --> 00:13:46,260
The next disorder is celiac disease.

259
00:13:46,400 --> 00:13:50,160
This is immune-mediated damage of the small bowel villi, so that's the key thing.

260
00:13:50,200 --> 00:13:53,880
You're going to knock out the small bowel villi, and it's due to gluten exposure.

261
00:13:54,240 --> 00:13:56,600
Basically, this is a hypersensitivity.

262
00:13:56,600 --> 00:14:00,780
There is an antigen out in the environment that, when it hits the bowel,

263
00:14:01,100 --> 00:14:03,320
results in destruction of the small bowel villi.

264
00:14:03,820 --> 00:14:09,680
It is associated with HLA-DQ2 and HLA-DQ8.

265
00:14:10,380 --> 00:14:15,320
Now, the key component that is going to result in this hypersensitivity is something called gluten,

266
00:14:15,680 --> 00:14:20,120
and gluten is present in wheat and grains and is in very many foods that we consume.

267
00:14:20,300 --> 00:14:25,120
The most pathologic component of gluten, you have to know for examination purposes, is gliadin,

268
00:14:25,120 --> 00:14:28,280
and you also need to know that gliadin gets deamidated.

269
00:14:28,680 --> 00:14:32,300
Now, when this occurs, it occurs by a tissue transglutaminase.

270
00:14:32,700 --> 00:14:37,060
Now, this changed or altered gliadin is then grabbed by the antigen-presenting cells

271
00:14:37,060 --> 00:14:41,440
and then presented via MHC class II to the helper T cells,

272
00:14:41,700 --> 00:14:44,340
and it's the helper T cells that mediate the tissue damage.

273
00:14:44,600 --> 00:14:47,200
So, it's the T cells that are creating the problem here.

274
00:14:47,740 --> 00:14:50,560
Now, clinically, this can have a very broad presentation,

275
00:14:50,560 --> 00:14:55,800
but the classic finding in children would be abdominal distension, diarrhea, and failure to thrive,

276
00:14:56,120 --> 00:14:58,940
and in adults, it would be chronic diarrhea and bloating.

277
00:14:59,480 --> 00:15:05,620
So, these are the ways by which we would classically see a presentation of this particular disorder.

278
00:15:06,060 --> 00:15:09,620
Now, one associated finding is something called dermatitis repetiformis,

279
00:15:09,620 --> 00:15:12,400
and the idea here is that if we draw a picture of skin,

280
00:15:12,860 --> 00:15:15,200
now, this is the epidermis up here,

281
00:15:15,560 --> 00:15:16,980
all right, so this is the epidermis,

282
00:15:17,200 --> 00:15:18,520
and underneath it is the dermis,

283
00:15:18,560 --> 00:15:20,540
and remember that we have dermal papillae here.

284
00:15:20,980 --> 00:15:24,420
Now, recall that the blood supply of the skin is all in the dermis.

285
00:15:24,520 --> 00:15:26,540
There are no blood vessels in the epidermis.

286
00:15:26,740 --> 00:15:28,500
So, as blood vessels come up here,

287
00:15:28,940 --> 00:15:31,000
now, one of the things that can happen in this disorder

288
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:35,460
is you can get deposition of IgA at the tips of these dermal papillae.

289
00:15:35,460 --> 00:15:39,040
Now, the deposition of IgA at the tips of the dermal papillae

290
00:15:39,040 --> 00:15:41,520
will result in destruction of the connection

291
00:15:41,520 --> 00:15:44,320
between the epidermis and the dermis at that point,

292
00:15:44,440 --> 00:15:47,760
so that what you'll get is a little blister or vesicle right here

293
00:15:47,760 --> 00:15:49,140
at the tip of the dermal papillae,

294
00:15:49,260 --> 00:15:54,720
and then you'll get another tiny little vesicle here,

295
00:15:54,860 --> 00:15:57,280
and then another tiny vesicle at the dermal papillae here.

296
00:15:57,620 --> 00:16:00,020
Now, these are little tiny vesicles, so they look like herpes,

297
00:16:00,500 --> 00:16:02,980
and so that's why it's called herpetiformis.

298
00:16:02,980 --> 00:16:07,820
You get very similar herpes looking like blisters,

299
00:16:08,400 --> 00:16:12,180
and so what's important to note is that it's due to IgA deposition

300
00:16:12,180 --> 00:16:13,660
at the tips of the dermal papillae,

301
00:16:13,900 --> 00:16:16,560
and that it resolves with a gluten-free diet.

302
00:16:17,380 --> 00:16:20,220
Now, the laboratory findings are that you get IgA antibodies

303
00:16:20,220 --> 00:16:21,480
against a few things.

304
00:16:21,560 --> 00:16:22,740
Number one, endomysium.

305
00:16:22,940 --> 00:16:25,020
Endomysium is the connective tissue

306
00:16:25,020 --> 00:16:27,700
that ensheets each individual muscle fiber.

307
00:16:28,120 --> 00:16:31,560
In this particular disorder, there is no problem with the endomysium,

308
00:16:31,560 --> 00:16:34,600
but it so happens that they develop antibodies against endomysium,

309
00:16:34,700 --> 00:16:36,240
and so that's one thing we can test for.

310
00:16:36,600 --> 00:16:39,140
They also can get IgA against tissue transglutaminase,

311
00:16:39,340 --> 00:16:41,040
or IgA against gliadin.

312
00:16:41,180 --> 00:16:43,740
So these are the classic laboratory findings.

313
00:16:44,080 --> 00:16:46,320
Now, what's interesting about patients with celiac disease

314
00:16:46,320 --> 00:16:49,280
is that a subset of these patients can be IgA deficient.

315
00:16:49,640 --> 00:16:54,460
Remember that when we talked about IgA deficiency in your immune lecture,

316
00:16:54,460 --> 00:16:58,440
I mentioned at that point that there's an increased incidence of IgA deficiency

317
00:16:58,440 --> 00:17:00,100
in patients with celiac disease.

318
00:17:00,500 --> 00:17:02,080
And so if they're IgA deficient,

319
00:17:02,140 --> 00:17:04,480
then you're not going to be able to find the IgA antibodies.

320
00:17:04,700 --> 00:17:08,580
And so it's also important to test for IgG antibodies

321
00:17:08,580 --> 00:17:10,680
because those would be useful for diagnosis

322
00:17:10,680 --> 00:17:13,000
in patients that have an IgA deficiency.

323
00:17:13,160 --> 00:17:14,480
Obviously, if you have an IgA deficiency,

324
00:17:14,620 --> 00:17:17,460
you won't have IgA against these particular antigens.

325
00:17:17,460 --> 00:17:21,020
And so in those patients, we want to look for IgG antibodies

326
00:17:21,020 --> 00:17:24,280
against, for example, tissue transglutaminase.

327
00:17:24,280 --> 00:17:25,360
This is just one example.

328
00:17:25,980 --> 00:17:27,440
Now, the way by which we make the diagnosis

329
00:17:27,440 --> 00:17:29,540
is also to do a duodenal biopsy.

330
00:17:29,880 --> 00:17:31,820
The biopsy will show flattening of the villi

331
00:17:31,820 --> 00:17:33,280
and hyperplasia of the crypts.

332
00:17:33,540 --> 00:17:34,700
Let's go back and remind ourselves

333
00:17:34,700 --> 00:17:36,180
that this is the small bowel surface.

334
00:17:36,400 --> 00:17:38,160
You've got these villi that go up,

335
00:17:38,180 --> 00:17:40,080
and you've got these tiny crypts that come down.

336
00:17:40,380 --> 00:17:43,580
So villi that go up, and then tiny crypts that come down.

337
00:17:43,960 --> 00:17:45,360
Now, what happens in this disorder

338
00:17:45,360 --> 00:17:46,940
is you get flattening of these villi,

339
00:17:46,940 --> 00:17:48,200
so you're going to lose these villi.

340
00:17:48,440 --> 00:17:50,500
You're going to get hyperplasia of these crypts,

341
00:17:50,520 --> 00:17:51,940
so the crypts are going to become deeper,

342
00:17:52,220 --> 00:17:53,740
and then you're going to see lymphocytes

343
00:17:53,740 --> 00:17:55,240
within the epithelium.

344
00:17:55,340 --> 00:17:56,580
So you get an increased number

345
00:17:56,580 --> 00:17:58,360
of intraepithelial lymphocytes.

346
00:17:58,700 --> 00:17:59,760
And here's what it looks like.

347
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:00,900
You've got flattening.

348
00:18:00,960 --> 00:18:01,620
Here's the surface.

349
00:18:01,760 --> 00:18:02,600
There are no villi,

350
00:18:02,660 --> 00:18:03,880
so you've got flattening of the villi,

351
00:18:03,940 --> 00:18:06,320
but you've got hyperplasia of these crypts,

352
00:18:06,320 --> 00:18:08,320
and then you've got all these lymphocytes present.

353
00:18:08,800 --> 00:18:10,300
Now, in particular, you would see them

354
00:18:10,300 --> 00:18:12,580
within the epithelium up here.

355
00:18:13,760 --> 00:18:15,700
Now, this is the normal histology,

356
00:18:15,700 --> 00:18:16,620
just to remind you that

357
00:18:16,620 --> 00:18:18,620
you normally should have these nice, tall villi,

358
00:18:18,760 --> 00:18:20,820
and then these crypts should be very minimal.

359
00:18:21,300 --> 00:18:22,240
All right, and so, again,

360
00:18:22,300 --> 00:18:26,100
we're losing all of these villi in celiac disease.

361
00:18:26,580 --> 00:18:27,800
Now, one more important point

362
00:18:27,800 --> 00:18:28,940
that I make here in the notes,

363
00:18:29,060 --> 00:18:30,440
and that is the distribution,

364
00:18:30,620 --> 00:18:31,680
which is super high yield.

365
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:34,140
The damage is mostly in the duodenum,

366
00:18:34,400 --> 00:18:37,120
and the jejunum and ilium are less involved.

367
00:18:37,440 --> 00:18:38,540
I can't repeat that enough.

368
00:18:38,660 --> 00:18:41,460
Remember that the damage is mostly in the duodenum,

369
00:18:41,460 --> 00:18:44,480
and the jejunum and ilium are less involved.

370
00:18:44,480 --> 00:18:46,620
All right, of course, as I've already mentioned,

371
00:18:46,680 --> 00:18:48,460
the symptoms resolve if you put the patient

372
00:18:48,460 --> 00:18:49,640
on a gluten-free diet.

373
00:18:49,960 --> 00:18:52,280
Now, let's say that on an examination in particular,

374
00:18:52,660 --> 00:18:54,860
the patient's on a gluten-free diet for many years,

375
00:18:54,960 --> 00:18:56,040
and they swear up and down

376
00:18:56,040 --> 00:18:57,800
that they've been maintaining that diet.

377
00:18:58,120 --> 00:19:00,640
Now, all of a sudden, they start getting disease.

378
00:19:00,820 --> 00:19:02,440
Again, refractory disease,

379
00:19:02,520 --> 00:19:04,880
despite the fact that they claim good dietary control,

380
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:07,320
and they've had good dietary control for several years.

381
00:19:07,800 --> 00:19:09,980
Then you need to think about two particular complications

382
00:19:09,980 --> 00:19:12,720
that could be leading to this refractory disease.

383
00:19:12,720 --> 00:19:14,360
The first would be that the patient

384
00:19:14,360 --> 00:19:16,140
could have developed small bowel carcinoma.

385
00:19:16,520 --> 00:19:17,640
So there's an increased risk

386
00:19:17,640 --> 00:19:19,600
for development of small bowel carcinoma

387
00:19:19,600 --> 00:19:21,300
in patients with celiac disease,

388
00:19:21,400 --> 00:19:23,560
especially with long-standing celiac disease.

389
00:19:24,120 --> 00:19:25,500
And then second, it's a possibility

390
00:19:25,500 --> 00:19:27,060
that the patient developed a lymphoma.

391
00:19:27,340 --> 00:19:28,300
But what's interesting here

392
00:19:28,300 --> 00:19:30,940
is that these patients can develop a T-cell lymphoma.

393
00:19:31,260 --> 00:19:32,020
And that's kind of rare

394
00:19:32,020 --> 00:19:34,520
because we haven't talked about many T-cell lymphomas.

395
00:19:34,580 --> 00:19:36,420
We've talked about a lot of B-cell lymphomas

396
00:19:36,420 --> 00:19:37,760
throughout this course.

397
00:19:38,160 --> 00:19:39,320
And so it's important to know

398
00:19:39,320 --> 00:19:42,540
that these patients can develop a T-cell lymphoma.

399
00:19:43,100 --> 00:19:45,480
And that T-cell lymphoma is also called EATL,

400
00:19:45,680 --> 00:19:48,080
E-A-T-L.

401
00:19:48,360 --> 00:19:49,200
And that stands for

402
00:19:49,200 --> 00:19:52,320
Enteropathy Associated T-cell Lymphoma.

403
00:19:52,600 --> 00:19:54,100
But anyway, I think the most important thing

404
00:19:54,100 --> 00:19:55,220
for you to note is that

405
00:19:55,220 --> 00:19:56,540
if there's refractory disease,

406
00:19:56,660 --> 00:19:58,460
think about cancer, think about lymphoma,

407
00:19:58,720 --> 00:20:00,580
and in particular, a T-cell lymphoma.

408
00:20:01,200 --> 00:20:04,020
Now, tropical sprue is another disorder

409
00:20:04,020 --> 00:20:04,880
that's very similar

410
00:20:04,880 --> 00:20:07,060
in that you get damage to the small bowel villi.

411
00:20:07,400 --> 00:20:09,460
And when you get damage to the small bowel villi,

412
00:20:09,460 --> 00:20:11,520
in this particular case,

413
00:20:11,600 --> 00:20:13,220
it's due to an unknown organism.

414
00:20:13,560 --> 00:20:15,120
Of course, it results in malabsorption.

415
00:20:15,240 --> 00:20:16,540
Whenever you knock out the villi,

416
00:20:16,620 --> 00:20:18,600
you're gonna get malabsorption and diarrhea.

417
00:20:19,200 --> 00:20:21,300
Now, this is very similar to celiac disease

418
00:20:21,300 --> 00:20:22,360
with a couple exceptions.

419
00:20:22,920 --> 00:20:23,880
Exception number one,

420
00:20:23,980 --> 00:20:25,920
it occurs in tropical regions like the Caribbean.

421
00:20:26,080 --> 00:20:26,500
And of course,

422
00:20:26,560 --> 00:20:28,380
that's because it's due to some infectious agent,

423
00:20:28,500 --> 00:20:29,820
and that's where the infectious agent

424
00:20:29,820 --> 00:20:30,780
tends to be located.

425
00:20:31,420 --> 00:20:32,060
Now, number two,

426
00:20:32,060 --> 00:20:34,240
it arises after infectious diarrhea.

427
00:20:34,240 --> 00:20:36,100
So, you clearly have an infection,

428
00:20:36,560 --> 00:20:39,300
and then the patient develops this tropical sprue.

429
00:20:39,660 --> 00:20:41,520
And it also responds to antibiotics,

430
00:20:41,680 --> 00:20:43,100
indicating that it's being driven

431
00:20:43,100 --> 00:20:44,520
by this infectious agent.

432
00:20:44,860 --> 00:20:46,380
But here's the super high yield point

433
00:20:46,380 --> 00:20:47,120
that I wanna make.

434
00:20:47,480 --> 00:20:49,180
And that is that in tropical sprue,

435
00:20:49,320 --> 00:20:52,400
the damage is more in the jejunum and the ileum,

436
00:20:52,660 --> 00:20:54,280
whereas in celiac disease,

437
00:20:54,620 --> 00:20:56,260
the damage was more in the duodenum.

438
00:20:56,580 --> 00:20:58,520
But the duodenum is less commonly involved

439
00:20:58,520 --> 00:21:00,180
in tropical sprue.

440
00:21:00,500 --> 00:21:01,880
So, the predominant disease

441
00:21:01,880 --> 00:21:03,480
is in the jejunum and the ileum.

442
00:21:03,860 --> 00:21:04,860
Now, because the disease

443
00:21:04,860 --> 00:21:06,860
is predominantly in the jejunum and the ileum,

444
00:21:06,940 --> 00:21:08,980
you can also get nutritional deficiencies

445
00:21:08,980 --> 00:21:10,800
based on what is occurring

446
00:21:10,800 --> 00:21:12,140
in the jejunum and the ileum.

447
00:21:12,480 --> 00:21:14,180
So, remember that in the jejunum,

448
00:21:14,520 --> 00:21:15,460
that is the key place

449
00:21:15,460 --> 00:21:17,460
where we absorb folic acid.

450
00:21:17,600 --> 00:21:19,220
So, we can get folic acid deficiency.

451
00:21:19,700 --> 00:21:22,100
And also, the ileum is the key place

452
00:21:22,100 --> 00:21:23,680
where we absorb vitamin B12.

453
00:21:23,680 --> 00:21:26,060
So, we can potentially develop,

454
00:21:26,160 --> 00:21:27,060
theoretically develop,

455
00:21:27,340 --> 00:21:29,000
a vitamin B12 deficiency.

456
00:21:30,320 --> 00:21:30,560
Okay.

457
00:21:30,560 --> 00:21:32,600
Next disorder is called Whipple's disease.

458
00:21:32,940 --> 00:21:34,500
This is a systemic disease

459
00:21:34,500 --> 00:21:37,340
in which you get macrophages

460
00:21:37,340 --> 00:21:39,740
loaded with T. whippelli organisms.

461
00:21:40,220 --> 00:21:41,460
Okay, what's the idea here?

462
00:21:41,620 --> 00:21:43,560
The patient gets a T. whippelli infection.

463
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:45,840
The macrophages begin to eat away

464
00:21:45,840 --> 00:21:46,800
at the T. whippelli,

465
00:21:46,920 --> 00:21:47,680
trying to kill them.

466
00:21:47,880 --> 00:21:49,020
But instead of killing them,

467
00:21:49,140 --> 00:21:50,800
they can only partially destroy them.

468
00:21:51,100 --> 00:21:53,020
And because they can only partially destroy them,

469
00:21:53,020 --> 00:21:54,800
the organism remains within

470
00:21:54,800 --> 00:21:57,000
the macrophage lysosomes.

471
00:21:57,640 --> 00:21:59,320
And so, that's an important consideration

472
00:21:59,320 --> 00:22:00,420
because in this disorder,

473
00:22:00,560 --> 00:22:02,200
you're going to get tons of macrophages

474
00:22:02,200 --> 00:22:03,880
loaded with the bugs.

475
00:22:04,380 --> 00:22:05,600
Now, the classic site of involvement

476
00:22:05,600 --> 00:22:08,060
is the lamina propria of the small bowel.

477
00:22:08,420 --> 00:22:09,260
And that's very important

478
00:22:09,260 --> 00:22:10,480
because that's then going to lead

479
00:22:10,480 --> 00:22:12,240
to the classic symptoms of this disorder,

480
00:22:12,360 --> 00:22:14,600
which is fat malabsorption and steatorrhea.

481
00:22:14,960 --> 00:22:16,280
But before we can understand that,

482
00:22:16,320 --> 00:22:17,800
we need to take a little step back.

483
00:22:18,040 --> 00:22:19,040
So, let's remind ourselves

484
00:22:19,040 --> 00:22:20,500
that when you think about the small bowel,

485
00:22:20,500 --> 00:22:21,860
you've got these villi, correct?

486
00:22:21,860 --> 00:22:24,120
And the villi have these enterocytes.

487
00:22:24,720 --> 00:22:26,300
And the enterocytes are sitting

488
00:22:26,300 --> 00:22:27,980
on a lamina propria.

489
00:22:28,080 --> 00:22:29,820
The connective tissue of the villus

490
00:22:29,820 --> 00:22:31,140
is called a lamina propria.

491
00:22:31,520 --> 00:22:33,240
So, that's the first point that I want to make.

492
00:22:33,520 --> 00:22:34,780
Now, I'm going to go back and draw that

493
00:22:34,780 --> 00:22:35,980
with that background in mind.

494
00:22:36,040 --> 00:22:37,100
So, you've got these enterocytes

495
00:22:37,100 --> 00:22:38,480
and then you've got the lamina propria.

496
00:22:38,840 --> 00:22:40,920
Now, let's talk a little bit about fat digestion.

497
00:22:41,440 --> 00:22:42,940
So, lipids are obviously broken down

498
00:22:42,940 --> 00:22:45,020
by lipases from the salaried gland

499
00:22:45,020 --> 00:22:46,780
and lipases from the pancreas.

500
00:22:46,780 --> 00:22:49,520
And then they're emulsified into smaller droplets

501
00:22:49,520 --> 00:22:51,640
in the stomach and the small bowel.

502
00:22:52,060 --> 00:22:53,240
And bile, of course, helps

503
00:22:53,240 --> 00:22:54,600
in that emulsification process.

504
00:22:55,040 --> 00:22:57,640
The end result is the production of a micelle

505
00:22:57,640 --> 00:23:00,660
that easily diffuses across the cellular membrane,

506
00:23:00,980 --> 00:23:04,880
which then brings in the fatty acids

507
00:23:04,880 --> 00:23:06,740
into the enterocyte.

508
00:23:07,200 --> 00:23:09,600
And once you have fatty acid within the enterocyte,

509
00:23:09,720 --> 00:23:10,960
then the next thing that's going to happen

510
00:23:10,960 --> 00:23:12,820
is you're going to repackage those fatty acids

511
00:23:12,820 --> 00:23:13,960
into chylomicrons.

512
00:23:14,380 --> 00:23:16,520
Now, chylomicrons are relatively big molecules

513
00:23:16,520 --> 00:23:19,060
and they're going to come out into the lamina propria.

514
00:23:19,280 --> 00:23:20,520
They can't go into the blood.

515
00:23:20,820 --> 00:23:22,540
And when they go into the lamina propria,

516
00:23:22,660 --> 00:23:23,840
they're going to be resorbed

517
00:23:23,840 --> 00:23:26,620
in these small little lymphatic spaces called lacteals.

518
00:23:27,620 --> 00:23:29,660
So, the chylomicrons are going to be brought in

519
00:23:29,660 --> 00:23:31,560
to these lacteals.

520
00:23:31,700 --> 00:23:33,680
And then, of course, they go into the lacteals,

521
00:23:33,800 --> 00:23:34,660
into the lymphatics,

522
00:23:34,740 --> 00:23:36,220
and the lymphatics eventually drain

523
00:23:36,220 --> 00:23:40,500
via the thoracic duct into the blood.

524
00:23:40,500 --> 00:23:42,520
So, that's the way by which the chylomicrons

525
00:23:42,520 --> 00:23:43,500
are brought into the blood.

526
00:23:43,600 --> 00:23:44,980
They can't go directly into the blood.

527
00:23:44,980 --> 00:23:46,420
They have to go into the lymphatics

528
00:23:46,420 --> 00:23:48,280
and then eventually they land in the blood

529
00:23:48,280 --> 00:23:49,380
because of their size.

530
00:23:49,940 --> 00:23:51,920
Now, what's going to happen in this particular disorder,

531
00:23:52,060 --> 00:23:52,940
which is Whipple's disease,

532
00:23:53,220 --> 00:23:55,360
is that you're going to get a ton of macrophages.

533
00:23:55,460 --> 00:23:57,340
So, let's just make them with squares, okay?

534
00:23:57,600 --> 00:23:59,440
You get a ton of macrophages present

535
00:23:59,440 --> 00:24:01,080
within this lamina propria.

536
00:24:01,420 --> 00:24:04,080
Now, all those macrophages are going to pile up

537
00:24:04,080 --> 00:24:05,620
and they're going to eventually compress

538
00:24:05,620 --> 00:24:07,380
these thin-walled lacteals.

539
00:24:07,900 --> 00:24:09,700
So, if you compress these thin-walled lacteals,

540
00:24:09,760 --> 00:24:11,620
you can't bring the chylomicrons in anymore.

541
00:24:11,620 --> 00:24:13,580
And if you can't bring in the chylomicrons,

542
00:24:13,680 --> 00:24:15,860
the patients are going to get a fat malabsorption.

543
00:24:16,080 --> 00:24:18,640
And that's exactly the mechanism by which they get

544
00:24:18,640 --> 00:24:20,940
fat malabsorption and steatorrhea.

545
00:24:21,480 --> 00:24:22,680
All right, here's what it looks like.

546
00:24:22,740 --> 00:24:24,480
This is the small bowel villus here.

547
00:24:24,880 --> 00:24:26,860
All right, and these are the enterocytes here.

548
00:24:27,120 --> 00:24:29,840
And you can see that there's all these foamy macrophages

549
00:24:29,840 --> 00:24:31,280
within the lamina propria,

550
00:24:31,380 --> 00:24:32,560
as I've just sort of outlined.

551
00:24:32,680 --> 00:24:34,920
Again, foamy macrophages in the lamina propria.

552
00:24:35,180 --> 00:24:37,120
Now, I could prove that these are macrophages

553
00:24:37,120 --> 00:24:38,160
loaded with the organisms

554
00:24:38,160 --> 00:24:40,700
by doing a special stain called a PAS stain,

555
00:24:40,780 --> 00:24:42,660
which would show me the little organisms.

556
00:24:42,900 --> 00:24:45,420
And this dark pink material within the macrophages

557
00:24:45,420 --> 00:24:48,400
indicates that I'm actually dealing with Whipple's disease

558
00:24:48,400 --> 00:24:50,340
because I can clearly see the little fragments

559
00:24:50,340 --> 00:24:51,360
of the organisms.

560
00:24:52,600 --> 00:24:54,380
Now, remember that this is a systemic disease,

561
00:24:54,480 --> 00:24:55,600
so that the most common site

562
00:24:55,600 --> 00:24:57,240
is the lamina propria of the small bowel.

563
00:24:57,500 --> 00:25:00,460
But it can also involve the joints giving you arthritis,

564
00:25:00,920 --> 00:25:02,140
in particular the synovium,

565
00:25:02,140 --> 00:25:04,880
the cardiac valves, the lymph nodes, and the CNS.

566
00:25:05,300 --> 00:25:07,180
So that's something to bear in the back of your mind.

567
00:25:07,980 --> 00:25:09,600
The next disorder that we're going to talk about

568
00:25:09,600 --> 00:25:11,300
is A-beta lipoproteinemia.

569
00:25:11,640 --> 00:25:12,500
A means without,

570
00:25:12,860 --> 00:25:15,060
and this is without your beta lipoproteins.

571
00:25:15,380 --> 00:25:16,700
Now, the idea here is that you get

572
00:25:16,700 --> 00:25:18,460
an autosomal recessive deficiency

573
00:25:18,460 --> 00:25:21,640
of apolipoprotein B48 and B100.

574
00:25:22,100 --> 00:25:23,720
Now, what's B48 important for?

575
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:26,160
You need B48 to make the chylomicron.

576
00:25:26,420 --> 00:25:27,700
So if you can't make the chylomicron,

577
00:25:27,780 --> 00:25:28,780
you can't absorb fat,

578
00:25:28,860 --> 00:25:30,200
and they're going to get a malabsorption.

579
00:25:30,200 --> 00:25:32,000
At the same time, you need B100

580
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:35,500
to be able to produce your VLDL and your LDL,

581
00:25:35,500 --> 00:25:37,300
and so therefore, these patients are going to have

582
00:25:37,300 --> 00:25:40,160
absent plasma VLDL and LDL.

583
00:25:40,840 --> 00:25:41,740
All right, moving along,

584
00:25:41,800 --> 00:25:43,440
we're going to talk about carcinoid tumor.

585
00:25:43,860 --> 00:25:45,900
Carcinoid tumor is a malignant proliferation

586
00:25:45,900 --> 00:25:47,040
of neuroendocrine cells.

587
00:25:47,200 --> 00:25:48,740
It's a low-grade malignancy.

588
00:25:49,240 --> 00:25:50,200
Now, let's remind ourselves

589
00:25:50,200 --> 00:25:51,440
that when you think about tumors

590
00:25:51,440 --> 00:25:52,460
of neuroendocrine cells,

591
00:25:52,680 --> 00:25:53,980
you can kind of have a spectrum.

592
00:25:54,300 --> 00:25:56,320
The worst tumor in the highest grade

593
00:25:56,320 --> 00:25:59,200
and the worst tumor of a neuroendocrine cell

594
00:25:59,200 --> 00:26:00,640
is called small cell carcinoma,

595
00:26:00,800 --> 00:26:01,880
and that occurred in the lung,

596
00:26:01,880 --> 00:26:03,060
and we've already talked about it,

597
00:26:03,420 --> 00:26:06,940
but remember that sort of the lowest form

598
00:26:06,940 --> 00:26:09,200
of malignancy of a neuroendocrine cell

599
00:26:09,200 --> 00:26:10,600
is called a carcinoid tumor.

600
00:26:11,560 --> 00:26:12,660
Now, a carcinoid tumor,

601
00:26:12,900 --> 00:26:13,800
it's important to note

602
00:26:13,800 --> 00:26:15,480
that those tumors are neuroendocrine,

603
00:26:15,580 --> 00:26:16,660
and so they're going to be filled

604
00:26:16,660 --> 00:26:17,940
with neuroendocrine granules,

605
00:26:18,140 --> 00:26:21,020
and very high yield that shows up on exams often

606
00:26:21,020 --> 00:26:23,200
is the fact that these tumor cells

607
00:26:23,200 --> 00:26:25,720
will be positive for an immunohistochemical stain

608
00:26:25,720 --> 00:26:27,200
called chromogranin.

609
00:26:27,460 --> 00:26:28,400
This is very high yield.

610
00:26:28,460 --> 00:26:29,640
It likes to show up on exams.

611
00:26:29,640 --> 00:26:31,580
So neuroendocrine tumors,

612
00:26:31,880 --> 00:26:33,520
in particular the carcinoid tumor,

613
00:26:33,620 --> 00:26:34,760
but this would also be true

614
00:26:34,760 --> 00:26:36,560
of a small cell carcinoma of the lung,

615
00:26:36,720 --> 00:26:38,760
would be positive for chromogranin.

616
00:26:39,440 --> 00:26:41,380
Now, carcinoid tumors can occur

617
00:26:41,380 --> 00:26:42,400
anywhere along the gut,

618
00:26:42,520 --> 00:26:44,380
but the small bowel is the most common site.

619
00:26:44,600 --> 00:26:45,720
The way I remember that

620
00:26:45,720 --> 00:26:48,320
is to remind myself that every site has cancer.

621
00:26:48,440 --> 00:26:49,880
For example, in esophagus,

622
00:26:49,960 --> 00:26:51,360
we talked about esophageal cancer.

623
00:26:51,600 --> 00:26:53,400
In the stomach, we talked about stomach cancer.

624
00:26:53,680 --> 00:26:54,400
When we do the colon,

625
00:26:54,500 --> 00:26:55,620
we'll talk about colon cancer,

626
00:26:55,800 --> 00:26:59,380
but there really is no common small bowel cancer.

627
00:26:59,380 --> 00:27:01,400
Remember that I told you earlier in our discussion

628
00:27:01,400 --> 00:27:03,900
that small bowel carcinoma is very rare.

629
00:27:04,460 --> 00:27:05,740
And so the way I remember this

630
00:27:05,740 --> 00:27:07,540
is that we've got to give the small bowel something,

631
00:27:07,760 --> 00:27:09,300
and so we'll give it the carcinoid tumor.

632
00:27:09,640 --> 00:27:11,220
So it's sort of just, I don't know,

633
00:27:11,240 --> 00:27:12,740
a funny way to remember something

634
00:27:12,740 --> 00:27:13,980
that you do need to know.

635
00:27:14,300 --> 00:27:15,340
Now, these normally grow

636
00:27:15,340 --> 00:27:17,540
as submucosal polyp-like nodules,

637
00:27:17,620 --> 00:27:18,600
and this is what it looks like.

638
00:27:18,620 --> 00:27:21,100
You get these little raised protrusions

639
00:27:21,100 --> 00:27:22,380
that are actually submucosal,

640
00:27:23,240 --> 00:27:25,520
but are creating these little polyps.

641
00:27:26,440 --> 00:27:28,220
Now, they often secrete serotonin,

642
00:27:28,260 --> 00:27:29,240
and this is very important.

643
00:27:29,620 --> 00:27:31,000
So when you have a carcinoid tumor,

644
00:27:31,100 --> 00:27:32,160
what's going to happen is,

645
00:27:32,200 --> 00:27:33,480
let's pretend that this is the tumor,

646
00:27:33,760 --> 00:27:36,180
the tumor is going to dump into the portal vein,

647
00:27:36,560 --> 00:27:38,200
and when it dumps into the portal vein,

648
00:27:38,280 --> 00:27:39,760
that is going to feed to the liver.

649
00:27:40,220 --> 00:27:41,640
And so when it feeds to the liver,

650
00:27:41,640 --> 00:27:44,940
then the liver is going to process that serotonin, right?

651
00:27:45,020 --> 00:27:46,320
Because, let's start over,

652
00:27:46,420 --> 00:27:47,840
the tumor secretes serotonin,

653
00:27:47,940 --> 00:27:49,180
dumps it into the portal vein,

654
00:27:49,240 --> 00:27:51,260
the portal vein will then send it to the liver,

655
00:27:51,260 --> 00:27:52,640
in the liver it will be processed

656
00:27:52,640 --> 00:27:56,620
by an enzyme called monoamine oxidase, MAO.

657
00:27:57,060 --> 00:27:59,060
Monoamine oxidase will break down the serotonin

658
00:27:59,060 --> 00:28:02,520
into 5-HIAA, which is a metabolite of serotonin,

659
00:28:02,640 --> 00:28:04,940
and that will then be excreted in the urine.

660
00:28:05,360 --> 00:28:06,360
Now, this is very important

661
00:28:06,360 --> 00:28:09,660
because when these tumors secrete serotonin,

662
00:28:09,940 --> 00:28:11,860
the only thing you're going to have at this point

663
00:28:11,860 --> 00:28:14,100
is an increase in 5-HIAA.

664
00:28:14,440 --> 00:28:16,720
There will not be carcinoid syndrome.

665
00:28:17,180 --> 00:28:19,960
Carcinoid syndrome requires that the serotonin

666
00:28:19,960 --> 00:28:21,600
go into the systemic circuit.

667
00:28:21,980 --> 00:28:23,200
However, in this particular case,

668
00:28:23,320 --> 00:28:26,580
all of the serotonin has been destroyed by the liver.

669
00:28:26,940 --> 00:28:29,180
Now, let's pretend that this carcinoid tumor

670
00:28:29,180 --> 00:28:32,740
metastasizes so that now the carcinoid tumor itself

671
00:28:32,740 --> 00:28:33,820
spreads to the liver.

672
00:28:33,960 --> 00:28:35,080
So here's the carcinoid tumor.

673
00:28:35,200 --> 00:28:37,040
It's now metastatic into the liver.

674
00:28:37,560 --> 00:28:39,300
Well, once it's metastatic into the liver,

675
00:28:39,600 --> 00:28:42,620
now the carcinoid tumor can dump its serotonin

676
00:28:42,620 --> 00:28:44,300
into the hepatic vein.

677
00:28:44,300 --> 00:28:47,120
And when it dumps the serotonin into the hepatic vein,

678
00:28:47,200 --> 00:28:48,960
it's important to note that the hepatic vein

679
00:28:48,960 --> 00:28:51,620
has tiny connections with the systemic circuit

680
00:28:51,620 --> 00:28:55,100
so that now this serotonin can actually get

681
00:28:55,100 --> 00:28:57,700
into the systemic circuit and hit places

682
00:28:57,700 --> 00:28:59,620
such as the lung and the skin

683
00:28:59,620 --> 00:29:03,100
and create the classic picture of carcinoid syndrome,

684
00:29:03,300 --> 00:29:05,680
which again is defined as bronchospasm,

685
00:29:06,080 --> 00:29:08,120
diarrhea, and flushing of the skin.

686
00:29:08,980 --> 00:29:11,420
All right, so it's important to note a general principle

687
00:29:11,420 --> 00:29:14,120
that only when you have metastasis to the liver

688
00:29:14,120 --> 00:29:17,560
will you actually get the carcinoid syndrome.

689
00:29:17,960 --> 00:29:19,580
I can't emphasize that enough.

690
00:29:19,900 --> 00:29:22,580
Now at the same time, because you've got metastasis to the liver

691
00:29:22,580 --> 00:29:24,180
and you're dumping it to the hepatic vein,

692
00:29:24,460 --> 00:29:27,200
the hepatic vein will eventually feed into the right heart

693
00:29:27,200 --> 00:29:28,360
via the vena cava.

694
00:29:28,700 --> 00:29:31,720
And when you get serotonin hitting the right heart,

695
00:29:31,960 --> 00:29:34,240
that's going to create fibrosis of the heart valves,

696
00:29:34,300 --> 00:29:36,240
and that's called carcinoid heart disease.

697
00:29:36,800 --> 00:29:39,380
So again, I'd like to highlight one central principle,

698
00:29:39,380 --> 00:29:41,660
and that is you don't get carcinoid syndrome

699
00:29:41,660 --> 00:29:43,680
and you don't get carcinoid heart disease

700
00:29:43,680 --> 00:29:47,200
until the tumor actually metastasizes to the liver.

701
00:29:47,900 --> 00:29:49,920
Carcinoid syndrome, of course, is bronchospasm,

702
00:29:50,060 --> 00:29:51,760
diarrhea, and flushing of the skin.

703
00:29:52,140 --> 00:29:53,400
These symptoms can be triggered

704
00:29:53,400 --> 00:29:56,000
by drinking of alcohol or emotional stress.

705
00:29:56,260 --> 00:29:58,000
Both alcohol and emotional stress

706
00:29:58,000 --> 00:30:00,560
can actually cause release of the hormone,

707
00:30:00,920 --> 00:30:04,360
of the serotonin from the tumor.

708
00:30:05,000 --> 00:30:07,500
At the same time, the patients can get carcinoid heart disease

709
00:30:07,500 --> 00:30:11,000
when there is metastasis of the carcinoid tumor

710
00:30:11,000 --> 00:30:12,280
to the liver,

711
00:30:12,620 --> 00:30:15,500
and that's going to result in a right-sided valvular fibrosis.

712
00:30:15,780 --> 00:30:17,800
It's important to note that the notion here

713
00:30:17,800 --> 00:30:19,560
is that you're going to get deposition of collagen

714
00:30:19,560 --> 00:30:20,600
within the valves.

715
00:30:20,900 --> 00:30:22,800
That deposition of collagen within the valves

716
00:30:22,800 --> 00:30:24,540
can lead to tricuspid regurgitation

717
00:30:24,540 --> 00:30:26,760
and pulmonary valve stenosis.

718
00:30:27,200 --> 00:30:28,800
So those are the two key complications.

719
00:30:29,280 --> 00:30:32,000
Now let me just make one point here very clear

720
00:30:32,000 --> 00:30:34,500
because it's also high yield for board examinations,

721
00:30:34,860 --> 00:30:37,240
and that is you don't get left-sided valvular disease.

722
00:30:37,780 --> 00:30:39,780
And the reason you don't get left-sided valvular disease

723
00:30:39,780 --> 00:30:42,320
is because when the serotonin goes into the right heart,

724
00:30:42,620 --> 00:30:44,640
it'll then get pumped into the lung,

725
00:30:44,900 --> 00:30:47,580
and the lung also has monoamine oxidase.

726
00:30:47,660 --> 00:30:49,040
Very important, very high yield.

727
00:30:49,340 --> 00:30:51,660
The lung also has monoamine oxidase,

728
00:30:52,000 --> 00:30:55,000
and therefore patients won't get left-sided valvular fibrosis

729
00:30:55,000 --> 00:30:57,540
because the serotonin will have been converted

730
00:30:57,540 --> 00:31:02,460
to 5-HIAA before it gets to the left side of the heart.

731
00:31:02,840 --> 00:31:05,740
All right, so you won't get left-sided valvular fibrosis.

732
00:31:05,920 --> 00:31:08,380
And that closes off our discussion on small bowel.

733
00:31:08,380 --> 00:31:10,360
Have a great day of life.

734
00:31:10,600 --> 00:31:11,160
Let's wait for years todop.

735
00:31:11,160 --> 00:31:12,220
Let's wait for years not to kill.

736
00:31:12,500 --> 00:31:14,200
Let's wait for years not to kill.

737
00:31:14,200 --> 00:31:15,120
Let's wait for years not to kill.

738
00:31:15,120 --> 00:31:16,700
All right, so Paul is not my nephew.

739
00:31:16,720 --> 00:31:17,180
Let's wait for years not to kill.

740
00:31:17,220 --> 00:31:19,280
Let's wait for years not to kill.

741
00:31:19,280 --> 00:31:21,100
Let's wait for years not to kill.

742
